course: dictionary
lesson: 1164
title: A brit, and reading big headlines
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A brit is a person from Britain. Brit is short and informal, so you will hear it in chat, jokes and the news. Americans and Australians use it a lot. Brits means British people in general, often as tourists, like in the phrase «Brits abroad».
explain: Read this headline: «Brits SIZZLE as Britain hotter than the Sahara». SIZZLE is in capital letters and is a dramatic word, like a sausage in a hot pan. Headlines are short and want to grab your attention, so they often make things sound bigger than they really are.
explain: Ask yourself what is really true. The headline might be true for one town, against one place, for one afternoon. The real facts are the actual temperature and what the weather service advises. When a headline sounds huge, look for the facts underneath it.
fact: A brit is a person from Britain, the country made up of England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.
fact: Brits means British people in general, often as tourists, as in «Brits abroad».
fact: Brit is an informal word, so it sounds friendly and chatty rather than formal.
fact: «SIZZLE» is a dramatic word that grabs attention in a headline.
fact: A headline can be true for a small case, but the facts are the actual numbers and the official advice.
exercise: «Brits abroad» means what? | Brown-skinned people from a hot country | *British people in general, often on holiday in other countries | Trains that run to the coast | People who sell cakes
exercise: Which word in «Brits SIZZLE as Britain hotter than the Sahara» is a dramatic headline word? | Brits | Sahara | *SIZZLE | Britain
exercise: The headline says Britain is hotter than the Sahara. What should you check to find out what is true? | Only the capital letters in the headline | *The actual temperature and the weather service's advice | Whether the Sahara has a football team | Whether the writer likes the sun
test: Why might a headline use the dramatic word SIZZLE? | *To grab attention with a dramatic word | To explain a spelling rule | To prove the writer lives in the Sahara | To give a boring weather report
test: Brit is an informal word. What does informal mean? | Used only by lawyers | Formal and serious, used only in exams | Written in a foreign language | *Relaxed and friendly, used in everyday chat
exam: Which statement is true? | Brits means one person from France | *A brit is a person from Britain | A brit is a type of hot desert | Brit is a formal word used only in law courts
